WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:10.936 --> 00:11.796 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

00:11.856 --> 00:14.186 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:14.776 --> 00:29.076 align:middle
I have a brief statement,
and then I'll be happy

00:29.456 --> 00:31.956 align:middle
to respond to your questions.

00:34.016 --> 00:37.576 align:middle
The job market remains strong,
the economy continues to expand,

00:38.336 --> 00:39.226 align:middle
and inflation appears

00:39.226 --> 00:42.476 align:middle
to be moving toward the FOMC's
2 percent longer-run goal.

00:43.746 --> 00:46.646 align:middle
As you already know, we decided
today to raise the target rate

00:46.646 --> 00:49.516 align:middle
for the federal funds rate
by 1/4 percentage point,

00:50.016 --> 00:52.956 align:middle
bringing it to 1-1/2to
1-3/4 percent.

00:54.196 --> 00:57.026 align:middle
This decision marks another
step in the ongoing process

00:57.026 --> 01:00.006 align:middle
of gradually scaling back
monetary policy accommodation--

01:00.186 --> 01:04.166 align:middle
a process that has been under
way for several years now.

01:05.566 --> 01:08.186 align:middle
Job gains averaged
240,000 per month

01:08.236 --> 01:10.696 align:middle
over the past three months,
well above the pace needed

01:10.696 --> 01:13.696 align:middle
in the longer run to absorb new
entrants into the labor force.

01:14.796 --> 01:18.266 align:middle
The unemployment rate remained
low in February at 4.1 percent,

01:18.476 --> 01:21.006 align:middle
while the labor force
participation rate moved higher.

01:21.986 --> 01:23.046 align:middle
Over the past four years,

01:23.046 --> 01:25.746 align:middle
the participation rate has
remained roughly unchanged.

01:26.496 --> 01:29.086 align:middle
That's a sign of improvement,
given that the aging

01:29.086 --> 01:31.226 align:middle
of our population is
putting downward pressure

01:31.226 --> 01:33.756 align:middle
on the participation
rate, and we expect

01:33.756 --> 01:35.326 align:middle
that the job market
will remain strong.

01:37.126 --> 01:39.116 align:middle
Although the growth rates
of household spending

01:39.116 --> 01:40.396 align:middle
and business investment appear

01:40.396 --> 01:42.056 align:middle
to have moderated
early this year,

01:42.206 --> 01:43.936 align:middle
gains in the fourth
quarter were strong

01:43.936 --> 01:46.036 align:middle
and the fundamentals
underpinning demand

01:46.036 --> 01:46.796 align:middle
remain solid.

01:47.536 --> 01:50.216 align:middle
Indeed, the economic outlook has
strengthened in recent months.

01:50.756 --> 01:52.736 align:middle
Several factors are
supporting the outlook:

01:52.936 --> 01:54.876 align:middle
Fiscal policy has
become more stimulative,

01:55.426 --> 01:58.366 align:middle
ongoing job gains are boosting
incomes and confidence,

01:58.896 --> 02:00.896 align:middle
foreign growth is on
a firm trajectory,

02:01.386 --> 02:03.726 align:middle
and overall financial
conditions remain accommodative.

02:04.256 --> 02:08.606 align:middle
Against this backdrop, inflation
remains below our 2 percent

02:08.606 --> 02:09.516 align:middle
longer-run objective.

02:11.056 --> 02:13.936 align:middle
Overall consumer prices, as
measured by the price index

02:13.936 --> 02:15.746 align:middle
for personal consumption
expenditures,

02:15.746 --> 02:19.486 align:middle
increased 1.7 percent in the
12 months ending in January.

02:20.296 --> 02:22.926 align:middle
The core price index, which
excludes the prices of energy

02:22.926 --> 02:24.966 align:middle
and food and is typically
a better indicator

02:24.966 --> 02:29.026 align:middle
of future inflation, rose 1.5
percent over the same period.

02:30.146 --> 02:32.946 align:middle
However, as we have noted
for some time, the shortfall

02:32.946 --> 02:35.686 align:middle
of inflation from 2 percent
reflects, at least partly,

02:36.086 --> 02:39.496 align:middle
some unusual price declines
that occurred nearly a year ago.

02:40.346 --> 02:42.986 align:middle
In coming months, as those
earlier declines drop

02:42.986 --> 02:46.006 align:middle
out of the calculation,
inflation should move up closer

02:46.006 --> 02:47.536 align:middle
to 2 percent and stabilize

02:47.566 --> 02:49.386 align:middle
around that level
over the medium term.

02:50.766 --> 02:53.566 align:middle
Of course, various forces will
continue to affect inflation.

02:53.816 --> 02:55.656 align:middle
At times it may be
above 2 percent,

02:55.776 --> 02:57.466 align:middle
just as at times
it may be below.

02:58.316 --> 03:00.546 align:middle
Our inflation objective
is symmetric in the sense

03:00.546 --> 03:03.076 align:middle
that we are trying to
prevent persistent deviations

03:03.076 --> 03:04.676 align:middle
from 2 percent in
either direction.

03:06.886 --> 03:09.266 align:middle
Based on the projections that
Committee participants submitted

03:09.266 --> 03:12.036 align:middle
for this meeting, the median
projection for the growth

03:12.106 --> 03:16.086 align:middle
of inflation-adjusted GDP
is 2.7 percent this year,

03:16.246 --> 03:19.576 align:middle
2.4 percent next year,
and 2 percent in 2020,

03:20.026 --> 03:22.686 align:middle
a bit above its estimated
longer-run rate.

03:23.206 --> 03:26.606 align:middle
The median projection
for unemployment--

03:26.666 --> 03:29.276 align:middle
for the unemployment rate
stands at 3.8 percent

03:29.276 --> 03:32.786 align:middle
in the fourth quarter of this
year and runs at 3.6 percent

03:32.786 --> 03:33.926 align:middle
over the next two years,

03:34.596 --> 03:37.036 align:middle
almost a percentage point
below the median estimate

03:37.036 --> 03:38.936 align:middle
of its longer-run normal rate.

03:40.026 --> 03:43.506 align:middle
Finally, the median inflation
projection is 1.9 percent this

03:43.506 --> 03:47.896 align:middle
year, 2 percent next year,
and 2.1 percent in 2020.

03:50.026 --> 03:51.816 align:middle
Compared with the
projections made in December,

03:52.136 --> 03:55.476 align:middle
real GDP growth is stronger,
the unemployment rate is lower,

03:55.886 --> 03:57.776 align:middle
and inflation is
slightly higher.

03:58.346 --> 04:01.556 align:middle
As I noted earlier,
today's decision

04:01.556 --> 04:04.816 align:middle
to raise the federal funds rate
is another step in the process

04:05.176 --> 04:08.276 align:middle
of gradually scaling back
monetary policy accommodation

04:08.276 --> 04:10.416 align:middle
as the economic expansion
continues.

04:11.336 --> 04:14.286 align:middle
This gradual process has
been under way for more

04:14.286 --> 04:15.856 align:middle
than two years, and
it has served--

04:15.856 --> 04:17.986 align:middle
and should continue to
serve-- the economy well.

04:18.556 --> 04:22.536 align:middle
In making our policy decisions
over the next few years,

04:22.596 --> 04:24.356 align:middle
we will continue to
aim for inflation

04:24.356 --> 04:27.626 align:middle
of 2 percent while sustaining
the economic expansion

04:27.626 --> 04:28.816 align:middle
and a strong labor market.

04:30.106 --> 04:32.616 align:middle
In the Committee's view,
further gradual increases

04:32.616 --> 04:35.206 align:middle
in the federal funds rate
will best promote these goals.

04:36.336 --> 04:39.306 align:middle
By contrast, raising rates too
slowly would raise the risk

04:39.306 --> 04:41.606 align:middle
that monetary policy would
need to tighten abruptly

04:41.606 --> 04:44.746 align:middle
down the road, which
could jeopardize the

04:44.746 --> 04:45.706 align:middle
economic expansion.

04:46.766 --> 04:47.836 align:middle
At the same time, we want

04:47.836 --> 04:50.176 align:middle
to avoid inflation running
persistently below our

04:50.176 --> 04:52.466 align:middle
objective, which could
leave us with less scope

04:52.466 --> 04:54.696 align:middle
to counter an economic
downturn in the future.

04:56.826 --> 04:59.006 align:middle
Participants' projections
of the appropriate path

04:59.006 --> 05:01.706 align:middle
for the federal funds rate
reflect our gradual approach.

05:02.666 --> 05:05.376 align:middle
The median projection for
the federal funds rate is 2.1

05:05.376 --> 05:09.166 align:middle
percent at the end of this year,
2.9 percent at the end of 2019,

05:09.166 --> 05:11.766 align:middle
and 3.4 percent at
the end of 2020.

05:12.736 --> 05:16.046 align:middle
By 2020, the median federal
funds rate is modestly

05:16.046 --> 05:18.186 align:middle
above its estimated
longer-run trend.

05:19.396 --> 05:21.656 align:middle
Most participants revised
up their projections

05:21.656 --> 05:24.106 align:middle
since December, although
the median projection

05:24.106 --> 05:25.686 align:middle
for this year did not change.

05:26.666 --> 05:30.476 align:middle
The medians for 2019 and
2020 are somewhat higher

05:30.476 --> 05:31.126 align:middle
than in December.

05:31.916 --> 05:34.666 align:middle
Of course, we'll be watching
how the economy evolves

05:34.666 --> 05:36.256 align:middle
in the months and
years ahead relative

05:36.256 --> 05:39.006 align:middle
to our maximum employment and
price stability objectives.

05:39.836 --> 05:41.976 align:middle
If the outlook changes,
we will adjust monetary

05:41.976 --> 05:43.076 align:middle
policy appropriately.

05:44.216 --> 05:45.726 align:middle
Finally, I'll note
that our program

05:45.726 --> 05:48.336 align:middle
for reducing our balance
sheet, which began in October,

05:48.656 --> 05:49.796 align:middle
is proceeding smoothly.

05:50.726 --> 05:53.266 align:middle
Barring a very significant
and unexpected weakening

05:53.266 --> 05:56.006 align:middle
in the outlook, we do not
intend to alter this program.

05:56.796 --> 05:58.816 align:middle
As we've said, changing
the target range

05:58.816 --> 06:01.016 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate is our primary means

06:01.016 --> 06:02.946 align:middle
of adjusting the stance
of monetary policy.

06:03.906 --> 06:06.116 align:middle
As always, the Committee
would be prepared

06:06.116 --> 06:07.836 align:middle
to use its full range of tools

06:07.836 --> 06:09.366 align:middle
if future economic
conditions were

06:09.366 --> 06:12.116 align:middle
to warrant a more
accommodative monetary policy

06:12.116 --> 06:15.346 align:middle
than can be achieved solely by
reducing the federal funds rate.

06:16.276 --> 06:19.616 align:middle
Thank you, and I'll be happy
to take your questions now.

06:20.266 --> 06:21.236 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

06:22.606 --> 06:25.686 align:middle
Mr. Chairman-- right,
the microphone.

06:26.686 --> 06:30.056 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, welcome.

06:30.876 --> 06:32.786 align:middle
Interesting changes
in the forecast.

06:33.336 --> 06:37.206 align:middle
A higher growth forecast a
full point above the long run,

06:37.976 --> 06:41.086 align:middle
lower unemployment
seven-tenths below the long run,

06:41.506 --> 06:43.866 align:middle
and yet very little
change in inflation.

06:43.866 --> 06:46.836 align:middle
What does that say about what
you and the Committee believe

06:46.836 --> 06:48.316 align:middle
about the inflation dynamic?

06:48.966 --> 06:51.246 align:middle
And how is it that,
in that context,

06:51.826 --> 06:54.096 align:middle
you justify three
rate hikes this year--

06:54.096 --> 06:55.526 align:middle
and I sense there'll
be three next year--

06:56.046 --> 06:58.396 align:middle
and a full $600 billion,
I guess,

06:58.396 --> 07:00.326 align:middle
annual rate decline
in the balance sheet?

07:00.846 --> 07:02.406 align:middle
Where's your biggest
concern here?

07:02.406 --> 07:05.576 align:middle
Is it in overkill when it
comes to rates or underkill?

07:05.576 --> 07:06.096 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

07:06.456 --> 07:08.676 align:middle
So, you're right that
the outlook did improve,

07:09.076 --> 07:11.636 align:middle
as I mentioned and as you--
as was in your question.

07:12.256 --> 07:14.836 align:middle
The Committee's estimates
of growth went up,

07:14.936 --> 07:17.026 align:middle
the Committee's estimates
of unemployment went down,

07:17.086 --> 07:19.176 align:middle
and there was a very slight
increase in inflation.

07:19.676 --> 07:21.646 align:middle
And I think that
reflects, essentially--

07:22.016 --> 07:26.006 align:middle
if you think back to the
era after the crisis,

07:26.006 --> 07:27.186 align:middle
unemployment was 10 percent.

07:27.226 --> 07:28.566 align:middle
It's now 4.1 percent.

07:28.926 --> 07:32.536 align:middle
You've only seen very gradual
upward pressures on inflation

07:32.536 --> 07:35.286 align:middle
and wages despite that
very large increase.

07:35.316 --> 07:38.066 align:middle
And that suggests that the
relationship between changes

07:38.066 --> 07:41.006 align:middle
in slack and inflation
is not so tight.

07:41.216 --> 07:43.926 align:middle
But, it has diminished,
but it's still there.

07:44.376 --> 07:48.076 align:middle
So I think when you see those
small changes in unemployment,

07:48.076 --> 07:51.246 align:middle
that simply reflects the,
you know, the flatness

07:51.246 --> 07:52.906 align:middle
of the Phillips curve,
if you will.

07:53.526 --> 07:53.986 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

07:54.766 --> 07:56.556 align:middle
And your biggest concern
or your biggest risk here--

07:56.556 --> 07:58.186 align:middle
is it doing too much,
doing too little?

07:58.186 --> 07:59.336 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

07:59.336 --> 08:01.146 align:middle
You know, I think we're
trying to, we're trying to be,

08:01.146 --> 08:02.246 align:middle
to take the middle ground there.

08:02.246 --> 08:06.106 align:middle
So, you know, on the one
hand, the risk would be

08:06.106 --> 08:08.886 align:middle
that we wait too long, and then
we have to raise rates quickly,

08:09.186 --> 08:11.576 align:middle
and that foreshortens
the expansion.

08:12.066 --> 08:13.216 align:middle
We don't want to do that.

08:13.406 --> 08:16.236 align:middle
The-- on the other side, if we--
if we raise rates too quickly,

08:16.606 --> 08:19.726 align:middle
inflation, then, really doesn't
get sustainably up to 2 percent,

08:19.976 --> 08:21.496 align:middle
and, that will hurt
us going forward.

08:21.496 --> 08:24.766 align:middle
We need that, and it's, you
know, we need to make sure

08:24.766 --> 08:27.626 align:middle
that inflation expectations
are anchored at 2 percent.

08:27.626 --> 08:29.766 align:middle
So we're trying to take
that middle ground,

08:29.766 --> 08:31.206 align:middle
and the Committee
continues to believe

08:31.206 --> 08:35.436 align:middle
that the middle ground consists
of further gradual increases

08:35.436 --> 08:36.676 align:middle
in the federal funds
rate as long

08:36.676 --> 08:38.706 align:middle
as the economy is
broadly on this path.

08:39.586 --> 08:40.596 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

08:42.666 --> 08:45.956 align:middle
Thanks. I'm Nick Timiraos
of the Wall Street Journal.

08:46.296 --> 08:47.546 align:middle
Chair Powell, I want to ask

08:47.546 --> 08:49.406 align:middle
about the symmetric
inflation target--

08:49.406 --> 08:51.196 align:middle
what you outlined
in your remarks

08:51.196 --> 08:53.076 align:middle
as preventing persistent
deviations.

08:53.446 --> 08:56.406 align:middle
I want to understand what
symmetry means in the context

08:56.406 --> 08:58.036 align:middle
of inflation being
allowed to run

08:58.036 --> 09:01.726 align:middle
above the 2 percent
target now that that's part

09:01.776 --> 09:05.786 align:middle
of the projection for-- at
least for core PCE next year.

09:05.846 --> 09:09.396 align:middle
How do you define symmetric
relative to the experience

09:09.396 --> 09:10.656 align:middle
of the past five years?

09:10.656 --> 09:13.866 align:middle
Would the Fed be willing to
accept the type of overshoot

09:13.866 --> 09:16.646 align:middle
of its target as it has
seen during the undershoot?

09:17.046 --> 09:20.046 align:middle
And how high above 2
percent is too high

09:20.446 --> 09:22.486 align:middle
to maintain a target
that is symmetric?

09:22.486 --> 09:25.436 align:middle
Is it 2-1/4, 2½, 2-3/4?

09:26.106 --> 09:27.336 align:middle
And also, for how long?

09:27.336 --> 09:28.026 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

09:28.026 --> 09:30.416 align:middle
You know, so as you noted, the--

09:30.526 --> 09:33.496 align:middle
what we've said in the
longer-run statement of goals

09:33.496 --> 09:36.116 align:middle
and monetary policy strategy
is that we would be concerned

09:36.506 --> 09:38.626 align:middle
with sustained or
persistent deviations

09:38.626 --> 09:40.156 align:middle
of inflation either
above or below.

09:40.446 --> 09:42.936 align:middle
We've also said-- in minutes
and in speeches and things

09:42.936 --> 09:45.566 align:middle
like that-- that that is
a symmetric objective.

09:45.766 --> 09:48.046 align:middle
So, that's how we think of it.

09:48.286 --> 09:51.916 align:middle
And, I think it's-- I wouldn't
characterize what we've done

09:51.916 --> 09:54.306 align:middle
over the last five years
as tolerating, you know,

09:54.536 --> 09:55.506 align:middle
an undershoot of inflation.

09:55.506 --> 09:57.926 align:middle
We were always pushing
toward 2 percent,

09:58.346 --> 10:00.526 align:middle
and I think that is
how we look at it.

10:00.526 --> 10:01.976 align:middle
I can't give you
an exact number.

10:01.976 --> 10:04.256 align:middle
It just, you know, it, we
haven't agreed on that.

10:04.256 --> 10:06.036 align:middle
It just is that we're
always going

10:06.036 --> 10:07.766 align:middle
to be seeking 2 percent
inflation.

10:08.626 --> 10:11.596 align:middle
And in doing that, we're going
to be considering, by the way,

10:11.596 --> 10:12.626 align:middle
the other side of the mandate.

10:12.666 --> 10:15.226 align:middle
You know, we have to balance
that against the deviation

10:15.226 --> 10:18.386 align:middle
of employment from--
unemployment from its goal.

10:18.926 --> 10:22.366 align:middle
And-- and also, just the
duration of it as well.

10:23.206 --> 10:24.946 align:middle
JIM PUZZANGHERA.

10:25.116 --> 10:25.856 align:middle
Hi, Mr. Chairman.

10:25.856 --> 10:27.566 align:middle
Jim Puzzanghera with
the L.A. Times.

10:28.246 --> 10:30.656 align:middle
Even with the extra fiscal
stimulus of the tax cuts,

10:30.656 --> 10:34.536 align:middle
your median growth projection
is just up to 2.7 percent,

10:34.926 --> 10:36.516 align:middle
and then-- this year--
and then drops back

10:36.516 --> 10:38.136 align:middle
down to 2 percent by 2020.

10:38.586 --> 10:40.026 align:middle
Is the continued
talk in Washington

10:40.026 --> 10:43.136 align:middle
about tax cuts bringing
growth to 3 percent or higher

10:43.136 --> 10:46.346 align:middle
on a sustained basis overselling
their potential impact?

10:47.806 --> 10:49.126 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

10:50.516 --> 10:52.706 align:middle
You know, let me say
what that really is.

10:52.706 --> 10:56.026 align:middle
So the Summary of Economic
Projections is really a

10:56.026 --> 10:58.756 align:middle
compilation of the
individual rate forecasts.

10:59.346 --> 11:01.836 align:middle
The Committee made really
one decision at this meeting,

11:01.836 --> 11:03.436 align:middle
and that was to raise
the federal funds rate

11:03.436 --> 11:04.596 align:middle
by 25 basis points.

11:05.666 --> 11:07.766 align:middle
The Summary of Economic
Projections is individual

11:07.836 --> 11:09.906 align:middle
forecasts compiled
and written down.

11:10.096 --> 11:13.376 align:middle
And, you've identified
the median as 2.7 percent.

11:13.376 --> 11:15.126 align:middle
I would-- and that's an
interesting statistic.

11:15.126 --> 11:19.336 align:middle
I would also say that the
central tendency is interesting.

11:19.336 --> 11:20.436 align:middle
The full range is interesting.

11:20.436 --> 11:23.816 align:middle
So you have a range of
views around the table.

11:23.816 --> 11:26.076 align:middle
One of the great
benefits of our system is

11:26.076 --> 11:29.356 align:middle
that we do have Reserve
Banks with their own staffs

11:29.356 --> 11:30.586 align:middle
and Governors with
different views,

11:30.586 --> 11:31.836 align:middle
and so we discuss these things.

11:31.836 --> 11:34.646 align:middle
So the Committee doesn't vote
or agree upon the medians,

11:35.326 --> 11:37.146 align:middle
so you're going to
have a range of views.

11:37.216 --> 11:38.416 align:middle
That's really all I can say--

11:38.416 --> 11:41.026 align:middle
2.7 just happens to
be the median for '18,

11:41.096 --> 11:44.646 align:middle
but it doesn't really say, you
know, what we think is possible.

11:45.186 --> 11:45.876 align:middle
JIM PUZZANGHERA.

11:46.036 --> 11:50.206 align:middle
But do you think you can get to
3 percent on a sustained basis?

11:50.206 --> 11:50.646 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

11:50.646 --> 11:52.356 align:middle
You know, I would say it's hard
to say, but it would take--

11:52.356 --> 11:55.666 align:middle
you know, that is well
above almost all estimates,

11:55.916 --> 11:58.146 align:middle
current estimates of
potential long-run growth.

11:58.146 --> 12:00.226 align:middle
It would take, you know,
significant increases

12:00.226 --> 12:02.926 align:middle
in productivity and labor force
participation to get there.

12:04.106 --> 12:08.306 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

12:08.646 --> 12:10.346 align:middle
Hi, Jeanna Smialek
with Bloomberg News.

12:11.136 --> 12:14.356 align:middle
So obviously you guys reduced
your NAIRU estimates slightly

12:14.356 --> 12:17.136 align:middle
today, but if you
continue to see job gains

12:17.136 --> 12:20.406 align:middle
around 200,000 a month, primary
labor participation rising

12:20.496 --> 12:23.686 align:middle
slightly, pretty
sustainably, you know,

12:23.686 --> 12:26.456 align:middle
moderate wage increases, at
what point are you guys going

12:26.456 --> 12:28.696 align:middle
to start to question whether
you're actually overshooting

12:28.696 --> 12:29.246 align:middle
full employment?

12:29.396 --> 12:30.736 align:middle
And, if you start
to question that,

12:30.736 --> 12:32.086 align:middle
how is that going
to impact policy?

12:33.056 --> 12:34.616 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

12:34.656 --> 12:38.056 align:middle
So I guess I'd start by
saying that the natural rate

12:38.056 --> 12:41.626 align:middle
of unemployment is not something
that we can observe directly.

12:42.216 --> 12:45.246 align:middle
We look at a wide range
of indicators on that--

12:45.556 --> 12:48.756 align:middle
you know, 15 or 20
of them at least.

12:48.936 --> 12:50.486 align:middle
And also, I think
we all understand

12:50.846 --> 12:52.946 align:middle
that any point estimate
is surrounded

12:52.946 --> 12:54.806 align:middle
by very wide uncertainty bands.

12:55.316 --> 12:59.696 align:middle
So as the-- as the actual
unemployment rate has declined

12:59.696 --> 13:03.396 align:middle
from 10 percent to 4.1 percent,
the Committee's median estimate

13:03.516 --> 13:06.166 align:middle
of the natural rate of
unemployment has also declined

13:06.166 --> 13:07.186 align:middle
by a full percentage point.

13:07.186 --> 13:08.736 align:middle
So the Committee--
and, I think, people--

13:09.846 --> 13:12.076 align:middle
are generally influenced
by data.

13:12.076 --> 13:14.876 align:middle
As they see unemployment
going lower, they're looking

13:15.046 --> 13:17.606 align:middle
at a variety of aspects
of the labor market,

13:17.606 --> 13:19.766 align:middle
including wage inflation
and price inflation.

13:20.286 --> 13:25.326 align:middle
So I don't think we, we-- we're
mindful of the uncertainty

13:25.326 --> 13:27.206 align:middle
of that, and we'll
be looking at that.

13:27.206 --> 13:29.666 align:middle
So, as-- if inflation-- if
unemployment does continue

13:29.666 --> 13:31.036 align:middle
to go down, we'll
continue to do that.

13:31.036 --> 13:35.126 align:middle
As I mentioned earlier,
there is no--

13:35.516 --> 13:38.316 align:middle
there's no sense in the
data that we're on the cusp

13:38.316 --> 13:39.896 align:middle
of an acceleration of inflation.

13:40.456 --> 13:43.846 align:middle
We have seen moderate increases
in wages and price inflation,

13:44.356 --> 13:46.146 align:middle
and we seem to be
seeing more of that.

13:46.146 --> 13:47.156 align:middle
We'll be alert to that.

13:47.286 --> 13:49.086 align:middle
I guess the idea--
the theory would be

13:49.086 --> 13:52.336 align:middle
that if you get below the
sustainable rate of unemployment

13:52.336 --> 13:53.986 align:middle
for a sustained period,

13:53.986 --> 13:55.686 align:middle
you would see an
acceleration of inflation.

13:55.686 --> 13:59.126 align:middle
So we would know that then,
and we're very alert to it,

13:59.226 --> 14:01.036 align:middle
but it's not something we
observe at the present.

14:02.046 --> 14:02.596 align:middle
SAM FLEMING.

14:03.296 --> 14:05.666 align:middle
Thanks very much.

14:05.696 --> 14:07.086 align:middle
Sam Fleming from
Financial Times.

14:07.126 --> 14:11.116 align:middle
You and other policymakers have
talked about headwinds turning

14:11.116 --> 14:12.686 align:middle
into tailwinds recently.

14:13.106 --> 14:16.976 align:middle
Does that imply that you think
now a higher neutral rate

14:17.266 --> 14:20.016 align:middle
of interest is operative
in the U.S. economy,

14:20.016 --> 14:22.476 align:middle
and how would you
assess the implications

14:22.536 --> 14:23.696 align:middle
for monetary policy?

14:23.696 --> 14:27.266 align:middle
And second question-- this
is obviously your first press

14:27.266 --> 14:29.266 align:middle
conference, so it's a little
early to ask this question,

14:29.266 --> 14:31.456 align:middle
but would you like to
do this more often?

14:34.156 --> 14:36.726 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIRMAN POWELL.

14:36.726 --> 14:39.676 align:middle
Longer-run values like the
neutral rate of interest,

14:39.896 --> 14:41.766 align:middle
the natural rate
of unemployment,

14:42.006 --> 14:45.866 align:middle
the potential growth rate
of the economy are pinned

14:45.866 --> 14:48.936 align:middle
down by slow-moving
forces over time.

14:48.936 --> 14:50.866 align:middle
So they don't-- they don't
move around very much.

14:50.866 --> 14:54.146 align:middle
So I wouldn't expect the
Committee's, you know,

14:54.256 --> 14:56.556 align:middle
predictions or projections
or estimates

14:56.556 --> 14:58.866 align:middle
of those values to move quickly.

14:59.586 --> 15:01.396 align:middle
They'll move slowly.

15:01.396 --> 15:04.336 align:middle
So is it possible
that the neutral rate

15:04.336 --> 15:07.846 align:middle
of interest would move up
because of, for example,

15:08.096 --> 15:09.156 align:middle
greater fiscal expansion?

15:09.156 --> 15:10.776 align:middle
There's literature
that says that.

15:10.856 --> 15:13.986 align:middle
There's-- there are reasons
that that might be the case.

15:14.336 --> 15:18.556 align:middle
In fact, it did tick up
one-tenth, as you all know,

15:18.556 --> 15:22.086 align:middle
in the-- in the median
at this time.

15:22.176 --> 15:25.606 align:middle
But it's-- I think,
generally speaking,

15:25.606 --> 15:27.536 align:middle
the Committee sees the
neutral rate of interest

15:27.536 --> 15:33.206 align:middle
as still quite low and is not--
is not seeing it as having moved

15:33.206 --> 15:35.686 align:middle
up and is open to the
possibility that it will.

15:35.686 --> 15:36.096 align:middle
SAM FLEMING.

15:36.816 --> 15:39.716 align:middle
And press conferences?

15:39.716 --> 15:40.046 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

15:40.436 --> 15:41.186 align:middle
Ah, press conferences.

15:41.396 --> 15:44.836 align:middle
So that's something
that I'm going

15:44.836 --> 15:45.886 align:middle
to be carefully considering.

15:45.886 --> 15:47.876 align:middle
I have not made a
decision about it.

15:47.876 --> 15:51.046 align:middle
My colleagues and I are
committed to communicating

15:51.046 --> 15:52.836 align:middle
as clearly as possible
about what we're doing

15:52.836 --> 15:54.506 align:middle
and why we're doing it.

15:54.506 --> 15:58.516 align:middle
I would want to think very
carefully about it and make sure

15:58.516 --> 16:02.126 align:middle
that no one would take more
frequent press conferences

16:02.126 --> 16:03.956 align:middle
as a signal of the
path of policy.

16:04.746 --> 16:07.356 align:middle
And so, it is something
that I'll be thinking about.

16:07.856 --> 16:10.446 align:middle
ADAM SHAPIRO.

16:12.026 --> 16:13.886 align:middle
Adam Shapiro with the
Fox Business Network.

16:13.976 --> 16:16.336 align:middle
You brought up the
fiscal stimulus

16:16.336 --> 16:19.526 align:middle
and the impact it's having, and
I was curious, how is the change

16:19.526 --> 16:21.886 align:middle
in the federal budget deficit--
because the stimulus is coming

16:21.886 --> 16:24.506 align:middle
with great debt-- has
it changed your approach

16:24.506 --> 16:25.876 align:middle
to how many securities
you're going to allow

16:25.876 --> 16:28.926 align:middle
to roll off the balance
sheet, and is there a level

16:28.926 --> 16:32.176 align:middle
of Treasury supply at which the
Fed would consider adjusting its

16:32.216 --> 16:33.266 align:middle
balance sheet roll-off,

16:33.396 --> 16:35.586 align:middle
given how much the U.S.
government's going to have

16:35.586 --> 16:36.996 align:middle
to borrow going forward?

16:36.996 --> 16:40.176 align:middle
And, then a second question,
things beyond your control--

16:40.176 --> 16:42.876 align:middle
the President is expected
to announce new tariffs

16:42.876 --> 16:46.386 align:middle
against China, and does the
Committee discuss what potential

16:46.386 --> 16:48.666 align:middle
impacts that could have
in regards to inflation?

16:48.666 --> 16:51.986 align:middle
And do you have a timeline as to
how you would respond to that?

16:53.606 --> 16:54.296 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

16:54.296 --> 16:56.296 align:middle
So, in terms of the balance
sheet, we've said that,

16:56.296 --> 16:58.416 align:middle
you know, we carefully
developed this plan.

16:58.416 --> 17:01.456 align:middle
We carefully socialized it in
a series of meetings last year.

17:01.456 --> 17:04.746 align:middle
We announced it, and we said
we wouldn't change it, really,

17:04.846 --> 17:07.716 align:middle
unless there were a significant
downturn that required,

17:08.236 --> 17:09.896 align:middle
you know, meaningful
reductions in interest rates.

17:09.896 --> 17:13.256 align:middle
And I have no inclination
to revisit that.

17:13.256 --> 17:15.896 align:middle
We're going to use monetary
policy as the principle tool

17:15.896 --> 17:18.696 align:middle
of adjusting, you
know, our policy.

17:19.466 --> 17:22.586 align:middle
So, on the second
question, on tariffs,

17:23.096 --> 17:28.876 align:middle
a number of participants in
this FOMC did bring up the issue

17:28.876 --> 17:34.126 align:middle
of tariffs, and if I could
summarize what came out of that,

17:34.126 --> 17:40.596 align:middle
it was, first, that there's no
thought, I think, that changes

17:40.596 --> 17:44.956 align:middle
in trade policy should have any
effect on the current outlook.

17:45.936 --> 17:48.256 align:middle
Second thing is, I would
say, is that a number

17:48.596 --> 17:50.786 align:middle
of participants reported in--

17:51.386 --> 17:54.676 align:middle
about their conversations
with business leaders

17:54.726 --> 17:56.506 align:middle
around the country and reported

17:56.506 --> 18:01.306 align:middle
that trade policy has become
a concern going forward

18:02.076 --> 18:03.366 align:middle
for that group.

18:03.786 --> 18:05.206 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

18:06.966 --> 18:08.846 align:middle
Marty Crutsinger with
the Associated Press.

18:09.196 --> 18:11.216 align:middle
You talked about the
fact that the Summary

18:11.216 --> 18:13.936 align:middle
of Economic Projections
is based on the outlook

18:13.936 --> 18:15.386 align:middle
of all the members of the FOMC.

18:15.466 --> 18:19.866 align:middle
Can you tell us anything,
though, about the staff forecast

18:19.866 --> 18:22.646 align:middle
and how that was
impacted by the--

18:22.646 --> 18:27.186 align:middle
we had a $1-1/2trillion tax
cut, we've had $300 billion

18:27.186 --> 18:29.026 align:middle
in increased government
spending--

18:29.276 --> 18:31.596 align:middle
the staff forecast that
you're working with,

18:31.916 --> 18:36.346 align:middle
are those reflected-- how
were they impacted by that?

18:36.346 --> 18:38.816 align:middle
And how did that
impact the discussions?

18:38.816 --> 18:39.716 align:middle
CHAIRIMAN POWELL.

18:40.056 --> 18:41.716 align:middle
So we look at the
staff forecasts

18:41.716 --> 18:43.876 align:middle
and the Board forecasts--
and, by the way,

18:43.876 --> 18:46.306 align:middle
there are 12 excellent
Reserve Bank staffs

18:46.786 --> 18:49.266 align:middle
who have their own views
and do their own work--

18:49.776 --> 18:51.666 align:middle
we look at those as
informing the decisions

18:51.666 --> 18:52.696 align:middle
of the policymakers.

18:53.186 --> 18:56.696 align:middle
So-- and it's really
the policymakers' views

18:57.176 --> 18:58.656 align:middle
that we talk about in the SEP.

18:58.656 --> 19:04.216 align:middle
We don't-- we don't, you know,
talk about the staff forecast.

19:04.216 --> 19:05.586 align:middle
It's a particular thing under--

19:05.586 --> 19:08.256 align:middle
done under particular
rules and circumstances.

19:08.256 --> 19:11.296 align:middle
And, really, it's the
policymakers that then take

19:11.296 --> 19:13.386 align:middle
that as input and
create their forecasts,

19:13.386 --> 19:14.456 align:middle
which are what drive policy.

19:14.676 --> 19:16.186 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

19:16.406 --> 19:18.506 align:middle
That had no impact, though?

19:19.456 --> 19:24.906 align:middle
It's such a-- that's a major
increase in government stimulus

19:25.056 --> 19:28.276 align:middle
that you're-- that had
to have had an impact

19:28.276 --> 19:30.676 align:middle
on the discussions, I
would think, though.

19:30.826 --> 19:31.446 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

19:31.446 --> 19:31.906 align:middle
Well, the-- you're right.

19:32.226 --> 19:36.966 align:middle
So fiscal stimulus is a, you
know, a meaningful input into,

19:37.156 --> 19:41.316 align:middle
you know, into the SEP and
particularly in the changes

19:41.316 --> 19:43.036 align:middle
over the course of last
year and this year.

19:43.666 --> 19:47.706 align:middle
And individual policymakers
went through, with their staffs,

19:48.086 --> 19:50.356 align:middle
a range of estimates,
a range of literature,

19:50.696 --> 19:53.046 align:middle
and did their own thinking and
came up with their own estimates

19:53.046 --> 19:54.646 align:middle
and submitted those
as their Summary

19:54.646 --> 19:57.006 align:middle
of Economic Projections--
as their projections.

19:57.326 --> 19:58.876 align:middle
Those are not the
staff's projections.

19:58.876 --> 19:59.816 align:middle
Those are our projections.

20:01.426 --> 20:04.306 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

20:04.746 --> 20:06.216 align:middle
Heather Long, Washington Post.

20:07.056 --> 20:10.806 align:middle
I-- what will it take to get
to four rate hikes this year?

20:10.806 --> 20:12.086 align:middle
It's clear from the dot plot

20:12.086 --> 20:15.206 align:middle
that many members do think
four would be appropriate,

20:15.206 --> 20:17.846 align:middle
but clearly something
was holding a number

20:17.846 --> 20:19.856 align:middle
of the FOMC members back.

20:19.856 --> 20:23.126 align:middle
Is it concerns about these
potential trade tariffs

20:23.316 --> 20:25.016 align:middle
that are going into effect?

20:25.016 --> 20:26.446 align:middle
Is it just that people
want to wait

20:26.446 --> 20:28.696 align:middle
and see a little longer
before they raise?

20:28.696 --> 20:29.916 align:middle
Can you give us some
more insight

20:29.916 --> 20:33.506 align:middle
into why people didn't go to
four this time-- the median--

20:33.506 --> 20:36.116 align:middle
even though the economic
projections look very healthy?

20:36.826 --> 20:37.546 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

20:37.546 --> 20:40.506 align:middle
I would go back to the
thought that, you know,

20:40.506 --> 20:43.286 align:middle
we made one decision at this
meeting, and that decision was

20:43.286 --> 20:46.906 align:middle
to raise the federal funds
rate by 25 basis points.

20:47.466 --> 20:50.966 align:middle
The projections are really
just individual projections

20:50.966 --> 20:55.696 align:middle
that are submitted
and then compiled.

20:55.696 --> 20:59.916 align:middle
And, you know, you're mentioning
the median as being, you know,

21:00.046 --> 21:04.366 align:middle
three and four being close, but,
you know, I think, like any set

21:04.366 --> 21:07.916 align:middle
of forecasts, those forecasts
will change over time,

21:08.016 --> 21:10.646 align:middle
and they'll change depending
on the way the outlook

21:10.646 --> 21:12.866 align:middle
for the economy changes.

21:12.866 --> 21:14.496 align:middle
So that's really all I can say.

21:14.496 --> 21:15.106 align:middle
It could change up.

21:15.106 --> 21:15.906 align:middle
It could change down.

21:15.906 --> 21:18.896 align:middle
I wouldn't want to-- for now,
these are the best forecasts

21:18.896 --> 21:20.906 align:middle
that people could
make, and, you know,

21:20.906 --> 21:23.526 align:middle
it could be that if the
economy's a little bit stronger

21:23.526 --> 21:27.026 align:middle
or a little bit weaker, then
the path could be a little less

21:27.026 --> 21:28.486 align:middle
gradual or a little
more gradual.

21:28.826 --> 21:29.046 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

21:29.046 --> 21:30.836 align:middle
So it wasn't one
factor that was pulling

21:30.836 --> 21:32.746 align:middle
at the heart of the discussion?

21:33.166 --> 21:34.046 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

21:35.516 --> 21:38.906 align:middle
Not, no. JIM TANKERSLEY.

21:38.906 --> 21:42.296 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, a quick follow
on the trade question,

21:42.296 --> 21:43.836 align:middle
and then a productivity
question.

21:44.246 --> 21:47.706 align:middle
On trade, what would have
to happen in policy--

21:47.706 --> 21:50.906 align:middle
and, in particular, in potential
retaliation from countries

21:50.906 --> 21:54.096 align:middle
around the world-- for trade
policy to become a concern

21:54.506 --> 21:56.026 align:middle
for the Committee's outlook?

21:56.516 --> 21:58.196 align:middle
And then, the second
question is,

21:58.226 --> 21:59.986 align:middle
you mentioned productivity
growth earlier.

22:00.246 --> 22:02.186 align:middle
What's your current
assessment of the path

22:02.186 --> 22:04.896 align:middle
of productivity growth, and
has anything in policy--

22:04.896 --> 22:09.596 align:middle
any policy change in recent
months changed that assessment?

22:09.926 --> 22:10.776 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

22:10.776 --> 22:13.346 align:middle
You know, for trade,
what I mentioned is

22:13.346 --> 22:17.946 align:middle
that our FOMC participants
reported in that they had heard,

22:18.556 --> 22:22.456 align:middle
you know, concerns, which
were relatively new, about--

22:22.686 --> 22:24.336 align:middle
about future trade
actions, really.

22:24.336 --> 22:25.736 align:middle
And they're seeing it

22:25.736 --> 22:27.246 align:middle
as a risk-to-the-outlook
kind of thing.

22:27.246 --> 22:29.526 align:middle
So-- and the kind of
things people are talking

22:29.526 --> 22:33.686 align:middle
about would be, you know,
more widespread retaliation

22:33.686 --> 22:36.796 align:middle
and more widespread actions
back-and-forth kind of thing.

22:37.376 --> 22:39.836 align:middle
I can't be any more
specific than that.

22:40.076 --> 22:41.716 align:middle
In terms of productivity--

22:42.316 --> 22:45.996 align:middle
productivity, as I'm sure
you know, has been very weak

22:45.996 --> 22:47.116 align:middle
since the financial crisis.

22:47.116 --> 22:49.566 align:middle
It's averaged only
about 0.5 percent a year

22:49.756 --> 22:52.876 align:middle
for the last six years, I guess.

22:53.636 --> 22:59.396 align:middle
So that's well below, you
know, longer-run averages,

22:59.526 --> 23:02.686 align:middle
and it would certainly be a
good thing to see it move up.

23:02.686 --> 23:03.956 align:middle
It has moved up just
a little bit

23:04.016 --> 23:05.466 align:middle
but not, kind of, decisively.

23:06.216 --> 23:10.116 align:middle
And, you know, you asked
me about fiscal policy

23:10.116 --> 23:13.446 align:middle
and the connection
to productivity.

23:13.776 --> 23:16.536 align:middle
I think in the tax
bill there's--

23:16.536 --> 23:18.136 align:middle
there are incentives
for-- there--

23:18.756 --> 23:21.806 align:middle
a tax cut bill that
allows expensing

23:21.806 --> 23:23.866 align:middle
of investments should
encourage additional investment

23:23.866 --> 23:25.126 align:middle
that should encourage
productivity.

23:25.826 --> 23:29.196 align:middle
In theory, an individual
tax bill

23:29.196 --> 23:31.606 align:middle
that lowers tax rates
should encourage more labor

23:31.606 --> 23:32.456 align:middle
force participation.

23:33.366 --> 23:38.246 align:middle
So I do think it's very
important that we have a focus

23:38.246 --> 23:39.656 align:middle
on productivity in the country.

23:39.656 --> 23:43.886 align:middle
It's not something that we
can really do at the Fed, but,

23:44.986 --> 23:46.366 align:middle
you know, we're certainly
hopeful

23:46.756 --> 23:48.846 align:middle
that there will be
supply-side effects

23:48.846 --> 23:50.456 align:middle
like that from the tax bill.

23:50.456 --> 23:54.146 align:middle
I think estimates are really
all over the place in terms

23:54.146 --> 23:58.586 align:middle
of the amount and
the timing of those.

23:59.156 --> 23:59.886 align:middle
JONATHAN SPICER.

24:01.176 --> 24:02.816 align:middle
Chairman Powell, Jonathan
Spicer with Reuters.

24:03.326 --> 24:06.696 align:middle
I take your caveats on
the dot plot, as it were,

24:06.696 --> 24:07.696 align:middle
but I'll ask another question.

24:07.696 --> 24:10.396 align:middle
In 2020, it shows

24:10.396 --> 24:13.276 align:middle
that policymakers expect the
policy rate to get up almost

24:13.276 --> 24:16.876 align:middle
to 3-1/2percent, which is quite
a ways-- or, definitively,

24:16.876 --> 24:18.796 align:middle
anyways-- above the
neutral rate.

24:19.226 --> 24:23.056 align:middle
Does that increase the risk
of a Fed-induced recession

24:23.146 --> 24:25.686 align:middle
and decrease the risk
of a soft landing?

24:27.216 --> 24:29.526 align:middle
Which is asking, in a way,
about the fiscal policy.

24:29.526 --> 24:34.226 align:middle
And, as a follow-up, is that
fiscal stimulus starting to lead

24:34.226 --> 24:37.626 align:middle
to questions within the
Fed around this narrative

24:38.236 --> 24:42.296 align:middle
of secular stagnation and the
economy entering a new sort

24:42.296 --> 24:43.446 align:middle
of "low rates" environment?

24:43.446 --> 24:44.856 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIRMAN POWELL.

24:44.996 --> 24:47.096 align:middle
You know, I would say that--

24:47.096 --> 24:49.766 align:middle
remember that forecasts three
years out-- so, you're right,

24:49.766 --> 24:56.036 align:middle
in 2020 there's a-- I think
it's 3.4 percent is the median

24:56.726 --> 25:01.556 align:middle
of the SEP dots for 2020,
which is 40 basis points

25:01.556 --> 25:03.696 align:middle
above the estimates
of the neutral rate,

25:03.696 --> 25:06.436 align:middle
which I would characterize as,
you know, modestly restrictive,

25:06.436 --> 25:08.676 align:middle
modestly tightening policy,

25:08.676 --> 25:10.076 align:middle
but that's three
years in the future.

25:10.076 --> 25:11.506 align:middle
It's highly uncertain.

25:12.336 --> 25:14.366 align:middle
You know, we don't have
the ability to see that far

25:14.366 --> 25:17.606 align:middle
into the future, so I really
wouldn't put a lot in that.

25:17.846 --> 25:19.016 align:middle
It could make sense.

25:19.016 --> 25:22.006 align:middle
You could imagine narratives
in which that would make sense,

25:22.066 --> 25:24.916 align:middle
but I honestly I wouldn't
put too much on that.

25:25.256 --> 25:26.966 align:middle
Sorry, your second question was?

25:29.276 --> 25:30.716 align:middle
JONATHAN SPICER.

25:31.906 --> 25:36.256 align:middle
The second question was
whether the secular stagnation

25:36.636 --> 25:38.606 align:middle
narrative-- whether fiscal
policy is being questioned

25:38.606 --> 25:40.096 align:middle
by the Fed around that?

25:40.096 --> 25:40.646 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

25:40.646 --> 25:43.466 align:middle
You know, we've been
through many years of growth

25:43.466 --> 25:46.776 align:middle
around 2 percent, and I've
given a number of public remarks

25:46.776 --> 25:48.936 align:middle
where I've called on, you
know, the country to focus more

25:48.936 --> 25:52.426 align:middle
on potential growth
and productivity

25:52.426 --> 25:56.226 align:middle
and labor force participation,
which drive potential growth.

25:56.226 --> 25:57.766 align:middle
So, you know, I think
it's really important

25:57.766 --> 25:59.996 align:middle
that we do something-- do
what we can as a country

25:59.996 --> 26:02.266 align:middle
to increase our potential
growth rate.

26:02.266 --> 26:05.226 align:middle
I would say in the bill that
passed Congress, the Tax Cuts

26:05.226 --> 26:06.556 align:middle
and Jobs Act, there
were elements

26:06.606 --> 26:08.416 align:middle
that should encourage
productivity, as I mentioned--

26:08.926 --> 26:11.596 align:middle
sorry, encourage investment,
which should help productivity,

26:11.596 --> 26:13.096 align:middle
encourage labor force
participation.

26:13.466 --> 26:15.116 align:middle
We don't know how big those
effects are going to be.

26:15.116 --> 26:16.516 align:middle
We don't know what
the timing would be.

26:16.516 --> 26:18.586 align:middle
I think, as you look around
the table at the FOMC,

26:18.586 --> 26:20.826 align:middle
there's a wide range
of views on the amount

26:20.826 --> 26:21.736 align:middle
and the timing of those.

26:22.166 --> 26:23.716 align:middle
I think all of us would
agree that we hope

26:23.756 --> 26:24.836 align:middle
that they will be large.

26:26.066 --> 26:27.046 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

26:27.876 --> 26:29.616 align:middle
Hi. Mike Derby from
Dow Jones Newswires.

26:29.616 --> 26:30.916 align:middle
I have a question
about the future

26:30.916 --> 26:32.896 align:middle
of the mechanics
of monetary policy.

26:33.166 --> 26:36.646 align:middle
I wanted to know whether you
favor sticking with the system

26:36.646 --> 26:38.816 align:middle
that you have now of keeping
interest on excess reserves

26:38.816 --> 26:41.266 align:middle
and reverse repo--
the reverse repo rate

26:41.266 --> 26:44.226 align:middle
to control interest rates--
or do you want to shift back

26:44.296 --> 26:46.746 align:middle
to the old way of doing
things at some point

26:46.746 --> 26:48.706 align:middle
of targeting the
fed funds market?

26:49.216 --> 26:51.546 align:middle
And do you have any
concern that if you do stick

26:51.546 --> 26:53.666 align:middle
with the current system
that, as rates rise,

26:53.986 --> 26:57.726 align:middle
that you might see issues
where the Fed is under--

26:57.726 --> 27:01.306 align:middle
is being criticized for paying
out ever larger shares of money

27:01.306 --> 27:03.856 align:middle
to banks to, you know,
to control interest rates

27:03.856 --> 27:05.906 align:middle
that some might perceive
as a subsidy to the banks

27:05.906 --> 27:07.176 align:middle
that are getting this money?

27:07.896 --> 27:08.766 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

27:09.096 --> 27:10.636 align:middle
Sure. Our current framework

27:10.636 --> 27:14.086 align:middle
for implementing monetary
policy is working very well.

27:14.296 --> 27:19.566 align:middle
We have excellent control
over rates, and it's working.

27:19.566 --> 27:23.466 align:middle
And it's-- you described
it accurately.

27:24.416 --> 27:28.676 align:middle
We haven't made a
decision to keep

27:28.726 --> 27:30.226 align:middle
that as our longer-run
framework.

27:30.816 --> 27:32.396 align:middle
We haven't really
addressed that question.

27:32.396 --> 27:33.776 align:middle
We've had meetings where
we've talked about it,

27:33.776 --> 27:35.796 align:middle
and we've agreed that
it's working well.

27:35.856 --> 27:37.656 align:middle
But it's-- and it's
not something I see us

27:37.656 --> 27:39.916 align:middle
as needing to urgently address.

27:40.336 --> 27:42.356 align:middle
I think we're continuing to
learn about this framework.

27:42.356 --> 27:44.616 align:middle
For example, one--
in the long-run,

27:45.186 --> 27:47.006 align:middle
the size of the balance
sheet is going to depend

27:47.006 --> 27:49.686 align:middle
on the public's demand
for our liabilities,

27:49.686 --> 27:51.226 align:middle
including currency and reserves.

27:51.226 --> 27:54.566 align:middle
So we don't know what the demand
is for reserves in a world

27:54.566 --> 28:00.216 align:middle
where you have-- you
know, you have regulations

28:00.216 --> 28:02.826 align:middle
that require banks to hold lots
of high-quality liquid assets,

28:02.826 --> 28:03.826 align:middle
and reserves are one of those.

28:03.826 --> 28:05.886 align:middle
So it's not something
we're looking

28:05.886 --> 28:07.446 align:middle
at resolving in the near term.

28:08.126 --> 28:10.816 align:middle
You mentioned the question of
interest on excess reserves,

28:10.816 --> 28:14.736 align:middle
and I think it's a little
bit of a misnomer to think

28:15.376 --> 28:18.276 align:middle
that there's a subsidy there.

28:18.496 --> 28:20.596 align:middle
We pay interest on
excess reserves.

28:20.706 --> 28:24.446 align:middle
We can't pay interest on
excess reserves that are--

28:24.446 --> 28:26.966 align:middle
is above the general level
of short-term interest rates.

28:26.966 --> 28:29.536 align:middle
So we're paying rates
that banks can get

28:30.116 --> 28:33.736 align:middle
from other interest
rates from other--

28:33.816 --> 28:36.026 align:middle
any other investment in the
short-term money markets.

28:36.546 --> 28:39.436 align:middle
In addition, remember
that those liabilities--

28:39.436 --> 28:41.216 align:middle
those are our liabilities.

28:41.246 --> 28:42.246 align:middle
The assets that we have

28:42.246 --> 28:44.046 align:middle
on the other side are
Treasury securities

28:44.046 --> 28:46.416 align:middle
and mortgage-backed securities,
which yield much higher

28:46.416 --> 28:47.346 align:middle
than interest on reserves.

28:47.346 --> 28:49.756 align:middle
So, in fact, it's not a subsidy,

28:49.756 --> 28:51.346 align:middle
and it's not a cost
to the taxpayer.

28:53.026 --> 28:54.166 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

28:55.696 --> 28:56.046 align:middle
Over here.

28:56.166 --> 28:57.316 align:middle
Thank you, Chairman.

28:57.966 --> 28:58.666 align:middle
Thank you very much.

28:58.666 --> 29:00.006 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

29:00.456 --> 29:03.406 align:middle
Several of your colleagues
recently have been speaking

29:03.406 --> 29:07.816 align:middle
and expressing concern
about financial imbalances

29:07.816 --> 29:09.866 align:middle
and rising signs of
financial imbalances.

29:10.696 --> 29:14.156 align:middle
I was wondering if you
could give us your view

29:14.696 --> 29:16.426 align:middle
on the asset markets.

29:16.426 --> 29:17.596 align:middle
Are-- do you see any bubbles?

29:18.076 --> 29:20.096 align:middle
And do you have the
tools you need,

29:20.096 --> 29:22.696 align:middle
you think, to combat those?

29:23.056 --> 29:23.606 align:middle
Thank you very much.

29:23.926 --> 29:25.706 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

29:26.436 --> 29:27.736 align:middle
Since the financial crisis,

29:27.736 --> 29:29.696 align:middle
we've been monitoring
financial conditions

29:29.696 --> 29:31.816 align:middle
and financial stability
issues very carefully,

29:31.816 --> 29:34.706 align:middle
and the FOMC receives
regular briefings

29:34.826 --> 29:39.186 align:middle
about the staff framework
and, sort of,

29:39.186 --> 29:41.666 align:middle
measures of various
aspects of financial--

29:42.446 --> 29:46.966 align:middle
financial stability
risks, and the current view

29:47.306 --> 29:48.696 align:middle
of the Committee is

29:48.766 --> 29:52.616 align:middle
that financial stability
vulnerabilities are moderate,

29:52.616 --> 29:52.976 align:middle
let's say.

29:53.486 --> 29:55.696 align:middle
And I'll go through a
couple of pieces of that.

29:55.986 --> 29:57.386 align:middle
So, if you look at
the banking system,

29:57.386 --> 29:59.706 align:middle
particularly the large
financial institutions,

29:59.706 --> 30:01.036 align:middle
you see higher capital.

30:01.386 --> 30:02.596 align:middle
You see much higher liquidity.

30:03.326 --> 30:05.966 align:middle
You see them more aware of
their risks and better able

30:05.966 --> 30:07.846 align:middle
to manage them with
stress testing.

30:08.456 --> 30:11.126 align:middle
And if one-- if something
does go wrong, you've got--

30:11.416 --> 30:14.456 align:middle
you've got better ability
to deal with the failure

30:14.456 --> 30:15.876 align:middle
of those institutions.

30:15.876 --> 30:17.536 align:middle
So, therefore, you
don't see high leverage.

30:17.536 --> 30:19.506 align:middle
You don't see excess risk-taking

30:20.176 --> 30:23.496 align:middle
in great quantity the way you
did see before the crisis.

30:23.496 --> 30:25.686 align:middle
If you look at households,
household balance sheets are

30:25.686 --> 30:27.336 align:middle
in much better condition.

30:28.116 --> 30:30.346 align:middle
If you look at nonfinancial
corporations, you see--

30:30.346 --> 30:35.056 align:middle
you do see relatively
elevated levels of borrowing,

30:35.156 --> 30:38.906 align:middle
but nothing that suggests,
you know, serious risks.

30:38.906 --> 30:40.726 align:middle
And, of course, default
rates are very low.

30:40.726 --> 30:43.226 align:middle
So those readings look okay.

30:43.726 --> 30:46.986 align:middle
I should mention also that for
large financial institutions,

30:47.086 --> 30:48.846 align:middle
they're no longer
funded by a lot

30:48.846 --> 30:50.346 align:middle
of short-term wholesale funding,

30:50.346 --> 30:51.696 align:middle
which can disappear
very quickly,

30:51.696 --> 30:55.086 align:middle
so they're far less
vulnerable to liquidity issues.

30:55.616 --> 30:57.266 align:middle
Overall, those aspects, I think,

30:57.486 --> 30:59.896 align:middle
suggest low levels
of vulnerability.

31:00.156 --> 31:02.916 align:middle
You identified the--
really, one area where--

31:03.486 --> 31:05.956 align:middle
which is an area of focus,
which is asset prices.

31:05.956 --> 31:09.566 align:middle
So, in some areas, asset
prices are elevated relative

31:09.566 --> 31:11.356 align:middle
to their longer-run
historical norms.

31:11.636 --> 31:13.656 align:middle
You can think of
some equity prices.

31:13.656 --> 31:15.916 align:middle
You can think of
commercial real estate prices

31:15.916 --> 31:16.766 align:middle
in certain markets.

31:17.276 --> 31:19.806 align:middle
But we don't see it in
housing, which is key.

31:20.416 --> 31:24.316 align:middle
And so, overall, if you
put all of that into a pie,

31:24.316 --> 31:27.326 align:middle
what you have is moderate
vulnerabilities, in our view.

31:27.946 --> 31:30.306 align:middle
In terms of whether we
have the tools, you know,

31:31.556 --> 31:35.176 align:middle
we have some tools, and I think
we certainly-- we use them.

31:35.176 --> 31:39.306 align:middle
We have-- I think the stress
test is a really important tool

31:39.306 --> 31:41.826 align:middle
that we have for the largest
financial institutions

31:41.826 --> 31:43.726 align:middle
and for the smaller
financial institutions.

31:43.726 --> 31:51.176 align:middle
We regularly use that to-- as
a way to test against various,

31:51.286 --> 31:52.406 align:middle
you know, market shocks,

31:52.406 --> 31:53.896 align:middle
certainly for the
larger institutions.

31:55.806 --> 31:57.146 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

31:58.736 --> 32:00.586 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
with Politico.

32:00.966 --> 32:04.706 align:middle
More on the regulatory
side, you know,

32:04.706 --> 32:08.646 align:middle
the Fed might soon be getting
more power to decide exactly

32:08.646 --> 32:11.576 align:middle
which regulations-- which
stricter regulations to apply

32:11.576 --> 32:15.676 align:middle
to banks with between $100
and $250 billion in assets.

32:15.756 --> 32:18.266 align:middle
And so I had a couple
of questions about that.

32:19.046 --> 32:23.886 align:middle
So, for CCAR, those
stress tests,

32:24.116 --> 32:27.636 align:middle
since that's based around, you
know, having a punitive penalty

32:27.636 --> 32:30.516 align:middle
of potentially being able
to restrict dividend payouts

32:30.516 --> 32:34.086 align:middle
or stock buybacks, is there any
kind of logistical challenge

32:34.086 --> 32:37.786 align:middle
that could be posed if you
don't have CCAR every year

32:37.956 --> 32:39.546 align:middle
for certain banks?

32:39.736 --> 32:43.196 align:middle
Is it possible to have CCAR
not on an annual basis?

32:43.196 --> 32:48.236 align:middle
And then, my other question is,
you know, you've talked a lot

32:48.236 --> 32:52.316 align:middle
about how size isn't the
only thing that causes banks

32:52.376 --> 32:54.396 align:middle
to pose systemic risk,
and I was wondering,

32:54.516 --> 32:56.736 align:middle
what other factors do you
think would cause a bank

32:57.036 --> 32:59.466 align:middle
to potentially pose
a systemic risk?

32:59.636 --> 33:00.696 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

33:00.696 --> 33:02.656 align:middle
Okay. So, this is a matter

33:02.656 --> 33:04.526 align:middle
that Congress has
under consideration.

33:05.106 --> 33:07.266 align:middle
It's not something--
so Congress is looking

33:07.266 --> 33:08.636 align:middle
at raising the threshold

33:08.636 --> 33:11.066 align:middle
for applying enhanced
financial standards to--

33:11.366 --> 33:14.346 align:middle
from $50 billion to $250
billion, while leaving us

33:14.346 --> 33:16.706 align:middle
with the ability to
reach below $250 billion

33:16.706 --> 33:19.446 align:middle
and apply those standards where
we think it's appropriate.

33:19.446 --> 33:22.006 align:middle
And, you know, we haven't been
shy about doing that, because,

33:22.186 --> 33:24.896 align:middle
of course, one of the eight
SIFIs is below 250 already.

33:24.896 --> 33:27.396 align:middle
So we are fully prepared
to do that.

33:27.646 --> 33:29.546 align:middle
But this is a decision that's
in the hands of Congress.

33:29.546 --> 33:31.556 align:middle
It's not something
that's been-- been taken.

33:32.016 --> 33:33.946 align:middle
The version of the
bill, I think,

33:33.946 --> 33:35.416 align:middle
that passed the Senate
did have--

33:35.566 --> 33:36.856 align:middle
did give us the ability

33:36.886 --> 33:41.206 align:middle
to do supervisory stress
tests periodically,

33:41.206 --> 33:43.506 align:middle
as opposed to annually,
is the language.

33:43.506 --> 33:45.436 align:middle
We haven't made any
decision about that at all.

33:45.876 --> 33:48.346 align:middle
We would want to think very
carefully about that, and,

33:49.546 --> 33:51.276 align:middle
you know, we would--
whatever we do decide to do,

33:51.276 --> 33:53.366 align:middle
we'd put that out for comment.

33:53.836 --> 33:56.446 align:middle
Is it, you know,
logistically possible?

33:56.446 --> 33:58.726 align:middle
I would think it would be, but
it's certainly not something

33:58.726 --> 34:00.646 align:middle
that we've decided to do.

34:01.326 --> 34:03.446 align:middle
And the second question
you had was?

34:04.006 --> 34:04.486 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

34:04.486 --> 34:07.296 align:middle
About what causes banks to
pose systemic risk beyond size.

34:08.126 --> 34:08.936 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

34:09.686 --> 34:12.416 align:middle
Sure. Well, it's-- I
guess the next thing

34:12.416 --> 34:13.816 align:middle
on the list would be activities.

34:14.416 --> 34:17.966 align:middle
So, you know, we-- regular
way commercial banks

34:17.966 --> 34:20.986 align:middle
that do deposit taking and
lending and don't speculate

34:20.986 --> 34:26.036 align:middle
in markets, you know,
that's one business model.

34:26.726 --> 34:29.766 align:middle
Another business model might
be, you know, if you think

34:29.766 --> 34:31.106 align:middle
of a hedge fund, right?

34:31.186 --> 34:32.986 align:middle
That's what they do.

34:32.986 --> 34:34.756 align:middle
They're speculating
on markets, and--

34:35.756 --> 34:37.906 align:middle
you know, so very
different business models

34:37.906 --> 34:40.146 align:middle
and different levels of
risk and that sort of thing.

34:40.146 --> 34:43.766 align:middle
So, that's-- that would be
the next thing on the list.

34:44.856 --> 34:46.976 align:middle
I mean, there would be a
range of things, you know--

34:46.976 --> 34:48.906 align:middle
how well capitalized
is the institution,

34:49.716 --> 34:52.596 align:middle
but mainly those things.

34:53.446 --> 34:56.476 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

34:56.736 --> 34:59.256 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Television and Radio.

34:59.636 --> 35:03.376 align:middle
I'd like to go back to the
idea of the additional growth

35:03.376 --> 35:05.076 align:middle
that you're getting
from the fiscal package

35:05.076 --> 35:08.456 align:middle
and ask you how much you think
comes from the supply side

35:08.456 --> 35:10.296 align:middle
and how much from demand?

35:10.726 --> 35:13.086 align:middle
You use words like
"might" and "should"

35:13.306 --> 35:17.636 align:middle
about what results should
come from this process, but,

35:18.026 --> 35:21.326 align:middle
around the table, did any of the
presidents or Governors report

35:21.326 --> 35:23.696 align:middle
that businesses are actually
getting ready to spend?

35:23.696 --> 35:25.346 align:middle
Is this something
that's going to happen?

35:25.696 --> 35:28.266 align:middle
Do we know that there's going
to be a supply-side effect,

35:28.266 --> 35:30.516 align:middle
or are you just guessing
at this point?

35:32.146 --> 35:33.056 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

35:34.196 --> 35:37.296 align:middle
Remember that there are 15
FOMC participants, and we--

35:37.296 --> 35:39.506 align:middle
each of us has his
or her own forecast.

35:39.506 --> 35:42.636 align:middle
So there's a real diversity
of views on this, particularly

35:42.636 --> 35:43.696 align:middle
on these fiscal issues.

35:44.076 --> 35:50.096 align:middle
If I could try to summarize,
it would be that, I think,

35:50.286 --> 35:53.546 align:middle
broadly speaking, participants
believe there will be meaningful

35:53.546 --> 35:56.746 align:middle
increases in demand from
the new fiscal policies

35:57.266 --> 35:59.526 align:middle
for at least the next,
let's say, three years.

36:00.006 --> 36:04.336 align:middle
I think there's a
general view as well

36:04.866 --> 36:08.036 align:middle
that supply-side issues--

36:08.036 --> 36:10.146 align:middle
that there could be
supply-side effects as well,

36:10.506 --> 36:13.446 align:middle
and you would see that again
through higher investment,

36:13.806 --> 36:16.286 align:middle
driven by lower corporate
tax rates and the expensing

36:16.286 --> 36:18.096 align:middle
of some investment,
which would drive--

36:18.136 --> 36:19.486 align:middle
tend to drive productivity
over time.

36:19.486 --> 36:20.846 align:middle
If you make investment
more attractive,

36:21.276 --> 36:23.646 align:middle
companies should do more of it.

36:23.646 --> 36:24.476 align:middle
It's uncertain, though.

36:24.576 --> 36:27.106 align:middle
I mean, I spent many years of
my life working with companies

36:27.296 --> 36:30.276 align:middle
and discussing-- and, you know,
the cost of capital is one

36:30.276 --> 36:31.726 align:middle
of many factors that
they'll consider.

36:31.726 --> 36:33.916 align:middle
It isn't the only factor
or the principle factor,

36:34.026 --> 36:36.146 align:middle
but it should result
in more investment,

36:36.146 --> 36:37.986 align:middle
and investment should
drive productivity.

36:37.986 --> 36:41.286 align:middle
At the same time, the-- you
know, I mentioned labor supply.

36:41.286 --> 36:45.096 align:middle
You should see some labor
supply effects over time

36:45.096 --> 36:48.366 align:middle
from lower individual
effective tax rates.

36:49.296 --> 36:50.686 align:middle
The whole thing is
very uncertain,

36:50.916 --> 36:53.396 align:middle
and particularly the
supply-side effects should

36:53.486 --> 36:56.006 align:middle
and would be expected
to take longer to appear

36:56.846 --> 36:59.566 align:middle
and would be less
certain in amount.

36:59.726 --> 37:02.216 align:middle
So that's really as
good as you can go.

37:02.216 --> 37:04.246 align:middle
And, you know, we've all looked
at the literature carefully.

37:04.246 --> 37:05.096 align:middle
We've all thought about it.

37:05.096 --> 37:07.656 align:middle
We've discussed it with our
in-house experts and come

37:07.656 --> 37:11.056 align:middle
up with our own views, and
they're disparate, but that's--

37:11.056 --> 37:14.196 align:middle
I'd say that's about where
the-- where the views are.

37:15.396 --> 37:15.986 align:middle
DONNA BORAK.

37:17.986 --> 37:18.726 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

37:18.726 --> 37:20.366 align:middle
Donna Borak with CNN.

37:20.636 --> 37:23.756 align:middle
Just to go back to trade
policy, if you don't mind.

37:23.946 --> 37:27.536 align:middle
You said that participants
today generally are feeling

37:27.536 --> 37:29.856 align:middle
that the trade policy shouldn't
necessarily affect the current

37:29.856 --> 37:32.076 align:middle
economic outlook,
but I am wondering

37:32.446 --> 37:33.586 align:middle
if there was any discussion--

37:33.586 --> 37:35.936 align:middle
if there are fears about
inflationary effects,

37:36.046 --> 37:38.476 align:middle
should there be trade
barriers imposed?

37:39.016 --> 37:41.646 align:middle
And, secondly, you know,
we might be on the verge

37:41.646 --> 37:44.756 align:middle
of a tit-for-tat
trade war with China.

37:44.756 --> 37:46.486 align:middle
Wondering what your
thoughts are of how

37:46.486 --> 37:47.846 align:middle
that might dampen the economic--

37:47.846 --> 37:50.526 align:middle
the global economic outlook
going forward should that occur.

37:51.346 --> 37:53.456 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

37:53.606 --> 37:54.736 align:middle
You know, at this stage,

37:55.086 --> 38:00.446 align:middle
what our FOMC participants
discussed-- it wasn't-- it was--

38:00.446 --> 38:02.456 align:middle
it was just that
this is a new risk

38:02.516 --> 38:06.686 align:middle
that had been probably
a low-profile risk,

38:06.686 --> 38:08.266 align:middle
which has become, you know,

38:08.266 --> 38:10.346 align:middle
a more prominent
risk to the outlook.

38:10.676 --> 38:11.956 align:middle
That's really what
people were saying.

38:11.956 --> 38:14.396 align:middle
They didn't get into
talking about particular--

38:14.516 --> 38:16.346 align:middle
you know, whether it's
inflation or growth

38:16.346 --> 38:17.526 align:middle
or whatever it would be.

38:18.096 --> 38:21.416 align:middle
So that's not something
that did come up.

38:21.566 --> 38:25.326 align:middle
You know-- your second
question, sorry, was China?

38:25.696 --> 38:26.426 align:middle
DONNA BORAK.

38:26.466 --> 38:33.346 align:middle
Yes. I mean, in the event that
the United States and China--

38:33.346 --> 38:35.136 align:middle
in the event that the
U.S. and China end

38:35.136 --> 38:39.066 align:middle
up in a tit-for-tat trade war,
or any other retaliatory actions

38:39.066 --> 38:42.066 align:middle
that are imposed elsewhere
against the United States,

38:42.176 --> 38:44.146 align:middle
what that means for the
global economic outlook,

38:44.146 --> 38:46.026 align:middle
and whether that was
something that was discussed

38:46.026 --> 38:47.136 align:middle
and are you worried about.

38:49.256 --> 38:50.206 align:middle
CHAIMRAN POWELL.

38:50.206 --> 38:52.556 align:middle
That didn't-- it's not something
that came up in particular.

38:52.556 --> 38:55.166 align:middle
And, let me say, you know,
we don't do trade policy here

38:55.296 --> 38:57.816 align:middle
at the Fed, and, you know, I
would be reluctant to comment

38:57.816 --> 39:00.496 align:middle
on any particular situation
with any particular country.

39:03.236 --> 39:04.596 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

39:05.506 --> 39:07.416 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

39:07.676 --> 39:11.016 align:middle
Does the interest rate
hike today suggest

39:11.106 --> 39:12.676 align:middle
that Americans are
being paid enough?

39:12.786 --> 39:16.916 align:middle
Are you satisfied with the
rate of wage growth right now?

39:17.686 --> 39:19.666 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

39:20.036 --> 39:21.096 align:middle
As I mentioned, you know,

39:21.486 --> 39:26.326 align:middle
we've had unemployment decline
sharply since, I guess, 2010,

39:26.326 --> 39:29.716 align:middle
when it peaked at 10 percent
and down to 4.1 percent now,

39:29.716 --> 39:32.196 align:middle
and we've seen only
modest increases in wages.

39:32.196 --> 39:34.866 align:middle
So, on the one hand,
what wages should

39:34.866 --> 39:40.386 align:middle
in theory represent is inflation
plus productivity increases.

39:40.386 --> 39:44.106 align:middle
You should get paid for your
productivity plus inflation.

39:44.726 --> 39:46.636 align:middle
And productivity's
been very low.

39:46.846 --> 39:47.726 align:middle
Inflation's been low.

39:47.726 --> 39:50.976 align:middle
So these low wage increases--
in a sense, they do make sense

39:51.656 --> 39:52.936 align:middle
in that-- from that perspective.

39:53.016 --> 39:54.856 align:middle
On the other hand, as
the market has tightened,

39:54.856 --> 39:57.436 align:middle
as labor markets have
tightened, and we hear reports

39:57.436 --> 40:01.586 align:middle
of labor shortages and
we see that, you know,

40:01.586 --> 40:03.456 align:middle
groups of unemployed
are diminishing,

40:03.456 --> 40:06.036 align:middle
and the unemployment rate is
going down, we haven't seen,

40:07.166 --> 40:10.996 align:middle
you know, higher wages--
wages going up more.

40:10.996 --> 40:14.206 align:middle
And I would-- I think I've
been surprised by that,

40:14.376 --> 40:15.526 align:middle
and I think others have as well.

40:16.356 --> 40:17.756 align:middle
In terms of what's
the right level,

40:17.756 --> 40:19.516 align:middle
I don't think I have a view
on what the right level

40:19.516 --> 40:21.926 align:middle
of wages is, but
I think we will--

40:21.966 --> 40:24.776 align:middle
we will know that the labor
market is getting tight

40:25.446 --> 40:29.816 align:middle
when we do see a more
meaningful upward move in wages.

40:30.216 --> 40:34.386 align:middle
VIRGINIE MONTET.

40:35.046 --> 40:37.296 align:middle
Virginie Montet with
Agence France-Presse.

40:37.716 --> 40:44.826 align:middle
If the economy behaves
as the Fed is predicting,

40:45.186 --> 40:48.996 align:middle
by the time of the midterm
elections, the rates will be--

40:49.056 --> 40:53.486 align:middle
keep on rising, and the
prime rate will be above--

40:53.486 --> 40:57.236 align:middle
well above 5 percent, a
level not reached in 10 years

40:57.236 --> 40:59.256 align:middle
for what is a base
of consumer credit.

41:00.096 --> 41:04.306 align:middle
And we know that,
historically, raising rates--

41:04.306 --> 41:06.806 align:middle
especially in campaign times--

41:07.076 --> 41:10.886 align:middle
has never been very
popular in the White House.

41:10.886 --> 41:17.876 align:middle
Does this prospect of a
possible pressure and dichotomy

41:17.966 --> 41:21.266 align:middle
of concerns keep
you awake at night?

41:22.536 --> 41:22.846 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

41:22.946 --> 41:25.786 align:middle
No. That doesn't keep
me awake at night.

41:25.786 --> 41:27.356 align:middle
We don't consider
the election cycle.

41:27.356 --> 41:29.256 align:middle
You know, we-- we're
looking very carefully

41:29.256 --> 41:31.966 align:middle
at our assigned responsibilities
of maximum employment,

41:31.966 --> 41:34.736 align:middle
stable prices, and
that's really our focus.

41:34.826 --> 41:38.176 align:middle
You can count on us to
stay focused on the jobs

41:38.176 --> 41:40.276 align:middle
that Congress has
assigned us and count on us

41:40.276 --> 41:43.066 align:middle
to use the tools we
have to achieve them.

41:43.716 --> 41:45.016 align:middle
You're right, that's
the highest rate.

41:45.016 --> 41:46.356 align:middle
By the way, I'm hearing this,

41:46.356 --> 41:49.466 align:middle
the 10-year thing a few times
these days, and it's true

41:49.466 --> 41:50.956 align:middle
that rates are higher than
they've been in 10 years.

41:50.956 --> 41:53.746 align:middle
On the other hand, the economy's
healthier than it's been since--

41:53.916 --> 41:55.616 align:middle
since before the
financial crisis.

41:55.616 --> 41:58.046 align:middle
So, it's a healthier economy
than it's been in 10 years.

41:58.046 --> 41:59.086 align:middle
The financial crisis, of course,

41:59.086 --> 42:01.056 align:middle
was just really getting
going 10 years ago.

42:02.346 --> 42:03.286 align:middle
MYLES UDLAND.

42:06.136 --> 42:07.666 align:middle
Thanks. Myles Udland,
Yahoo Finance.

42:08.046 --> 42:10.556 align:middle
Chair Powell, I'm curious
if the Fed would be willing

42:10.556 --> 42:12.536 align:middle
to tolerate an inverted
yield curve.

42:12.586 --> 42:15.176 align:middle
We continue to see the
spread between the 2-year

42:15.176 --> 42:18.586 align:middle
and the 10-year tighten, even
with longer-term yields coming

42:18.586 --> 42:19.886 align:middle
up since the beginning
of the year.

42:19.886 --> 42:23.376 align:middle
This is a dynamic that has
typically preceded recessions,

42:23.376 --> 42:25.746 align:middle
and we're likely to see
shorter-term rates come

42:25.746 --> 42:28.286 align:middle
up as the Fed continues
to increase rates.

42:28.576 --> 42:30.416 align:middle
So, I'm just curious if you
guys have discussed that,

42:30.416 --> 42:32.606 align:middle
if you'd be willing to
push back against that,

42:32.606 --> 42:34.106 align:middle
or if that's a dynamic
you'd be comfortable with?

42:34.106 --> 42:35.146 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

42:35.416 --> 42:36.496 align:middle
You know, it's an
interesting question,

42:36.496 --> 42:38.086 align:middle
and there are a range
of views there.

42:38.256 --> 42:41.906 align:middle
I think it's true that
yield curves have tended

42:41.906 --> 42:45.066 align:middle
to predict recessions if you
look back over many cycles,

42:45.106 --> 42:47.116 align:middle
but a lot of that
was just situations

42:47.116 --> 42:50.506 align:middle
in which inflation was
allowed to get out of control,

42:50.506 --> 42:51.676 align:middle
and the Fed had to tighten,

42:51.676 --> 42:53.666 align:middle
and that put the
economy into a recession.

42:53.876 --> 42:57.636 align:middle
That's really not the situation
we're in now, so I don't know

42:57.636 --> 43:00.126 align:middle
that that's-- I don't
know that--

43:00.126 --> 43:03.116 align:middle
I don't think that recession
probabilities are particularly

43:03.116 --> 43:05.446 align:middle
high at the moment, any
higher than they normally are.

43:05.886 --> 43:07.986 align:middle
But, having said
that, I think it's--

43:08.256 --> 43:11.096 align:middle
there are good questions
about what a flat yield curve

43:11.096 --> 43:13.566 align:middle
or inverted yield curve
does to intermediation.

43:13.896 --> 43:17.056 align:middle
It's hard to find in the
research data, but nonetheless,

43:17.056 --> 43:20.736 align:middle
I think those are issues that
we'll be watching carefully.

43:22.676 --> 43:29.116 align:middle
Thank you very much.

